-- title:
--   Tate module of the points of a p-divisible group
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, a commutative ring $O$, a natural number $h$, and $H$ a $p$-divisible group of height $h$ over $O$ in the sense of the project's structure [`PDivisibleGroup`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199): a family of finite free cocommutative Hopf $O$-algebras $H.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with $\operatorname{finrank}_O H.\mathrm{level}\,v = p^{vh}$, together with surjective coalgebra–algebra maps $H.\mathrm{level}\,(v+1) \to H.\mathrm{level}\,v$ whose kernels are the ideals generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$. Let $L$ be a commutative $O$-algebra, and write $H.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ for the convolution group of $O$-algebra maps $H.\mathrm{level}\,v \to L$ and $H.\mathrm{Points}\,L$ for the direct limit of the groups $H.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ along the transition inclusions. Assume that for every $v$ the group $H.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ is finite of cardinality $p^{vh}$. Let $T = \mathtt{TateModule}\,p\,(H.\mathrm{Points}\,L)$ be the group of sequences $y : \mathbb{N} \to H.\mathrm{Points}\,L$ satisfying $p^n \cdot y_n = 0$ and $p \cdot y_{n+1} = y_n$ for all $n$, with its $\mathbb{Z}_p$-module structure. Then: (i) for every $v$ and every $x \in H.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ there is $y \in T$ whose $v$-th component is the image of $x$ in the direct limit; (ii) for every $v$ and every $y \in T$, the component $y_v$ vanishes if and only if $y = p^v \cdot z$ for some $z \in T$; (iii) $T$ is a free $\mathbb{Z}_p$-module; and (iv) its $\mathbb{Z}_p$-rank equals $h$.
--
--   This is the standard description of the Tate module of a $p$-divisible group of height $h$: $T_p$ is free of rank $h$ over $\mathbb{Z}_p$, and items (i) and (ii) together express the isomorphism $T/p^vT \cong H_v(L)$ via the $v$-th component map. It supplies the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-lattice underlying the $p$-adic representations attached to the $p$-divisible groups of the Néron models of modular curves, and is used in the analysis of level structures, torsion point counts and idempotents attached to those objects.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PDivisibleGroup_exists_tateModule_apply_eq_and_apply_eq_zero_iff_and_free_finrank_of_natCard_point_eq.lean

